molecules like cationic dyes (Basic Red 2, Rhodamine B, and Crystal Violet), anionic dyes (Methyl Blue, and Brilliant Blue G), and neutral dye (Rhodamine 6G) are difficult to be encapsulated into the neutral HSB-W1. On the contrary, the linear guest molecules such as fluorescent brightener (CBS-127, CBS-X, and KSN), coumarin derivatives (Coumarin 6, C6; 7-(diethylamino)-3-(2-thienyl)coumarin, C6a; etc.), DCM and DSM (Figure 2a; and Figure S4, Supporting Information) with blue, green, and red fluorescent emission, respectively, can impregnate into HSB-W1 easily, regardless of whether they are neutral or ionic. Therefore, the encapsulation process is guest-size-dependent, which is partly similar to the ion exchange processes reported previously.[14,27] By varying the concentration of the dye in DMF solution, the content of the encapsulated dye in HSB-W1 can be adjusted accordingly. Furthermore, the combined dyes can also be introduced and their contents in HSB-W1 could be tuned by using the same way. The PXRD patterns of HSB-W1, HSB-W1> dye, and HSB-W1> dyes are similar, which indicates that the introduction of guest molecules into HSB-W1 does not damage the crystalline structure (Figure 2b; and Figure S5, Supporting Information).

The DCM solution in ethanol displays a strong red emission around \(627\;\mathrm{nm}\) upon excitation at 471, 372, or \(352\;\mathrm{nm}\).[28] Thus DCM was chosen as the red-emitting encapsulant in our system. As expected, the emission band of HSB-W1>DCM is centered at about \(610\;\mathrm{nm}\) when excited at \(120{-}540\;\mathrm{nm}\), which is originated from DCM. The phenomenon is also indicated that the red-emitting DCM has been successfully introduced into HSB-W1. Furthermore, the intensity of the red emission can

