be tuned through adjusting the content of DCM in HSB-W1 via changing the concentration of their soak solution. As shown in Figure 3a, with the increase of the DCM content in HSB-W1, the blue emission of MOF host was weakened gradually, while the red emission of DCM was enhanced accordingly. The fluorescent change during the encapsulation process was also investigated by immersing HSB-W1 within the DCM solution for different time periods. As seen in Figure S6 in the Supporting Information, the intensity of the red emission band increased gradually with the increase of soaking time, while opposite response was observed for the blue emission. Those phenomena mean that the excitation energy transfer from MOF to guest dye molecules occurred, due to the overlap between the emission of HSB-W1 and the absorption of DCM.[25] When the DCM content in HSB-W1>DCM was up to \(0.04\;\mathrm{wt}\%\), the blue emission of MOF host began to vanish. The CIE coordinates for HSB-W1>DCM with DCM loading amount of \(0.31\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) shows red emission with CIE coordinates of (0.60, 0.38), close to those (0.66, 0.33) for the saturated red emitter. The quantum yields of HSB-W1>DCM (0.004–0.15 wt% DCM) were measured to be \(29.9{-}13.1\%\), which are much higher than that of the DCM powder (<1%) under excitation at \(365\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Table S3, Supporting Information). The enhancement of emission intensity and quantum efficiency may be caused by the confinement effect. When the guest DCM molecules are confined into HSB-W1, the confinement effect imposes strong restrictions on their mobility, and thereby suppresses their aggregation and the aggregation-caused quenching.[29] The study suggests that HSB-W1 can become a red phosphor by introducing red-emitting DCM dye.

The coumarin derivatives, C6 and C6a, in DMF solution display intense green emission bands centered at about 520 and \(510\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively, upon \(365\;\mathrm{nm}\) light excitation, which therefore could be used as the green-emitting encapsulant (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Fortunately, they can also be easily entered the pores of HSB-W1. The color of the MOF materials was changed from colorless to green quickly upon encapsulation, and a new strong green emission peak generated under excitation. We took coumarin C6 as the model molecule for systematical research. The blue emission of the MOF host was receded while the intensity of green emission was strengthened greatly with the increase the amount of guest C6 in HSB-W1, similar to the phenomena for HSB-W1>DCM. When the content of C6 exceeded \(0.02\;\mathrm{wt}\%\), the emission of MOF host disappeared (Figure 3b). The quantum yields of HSB-W1>C6 (0.003–0.04 wt% C6) were ranged from \(20.5\%\) to \(6.5\%\) (\(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=365\;\mathrm{nm}\)), while that of the pure C6 in solid state was \(18.8\%\) (Table S3, Supporting Information). The CIE coordinates for the green emission of HSB-W1>C6 (0.04 wt% C6) are (0.24, 0.65), close to those (0.21, 0.71) for the saturated green emitter. Therefore, coumarin C6 is a good dye guest to tune the fluorescent color of the MOF materials to green.

Encouraged by these results, the introduction of the blue-emitting guest molecules into HSB-W1 was further investigated, which is of significant importance for inducing white light emission. Fluorescent brighteners (CBS-127, CBS-X, and KSN) show strong blue fluorescence in both solid state and solution. Neutral CBS-127, KSN, and ionic CBS-X can be easily brought into the pores of HSB-W1, and the blue emission
