To obtain white-light, it is important that the overall emission profile should be as far as possible to the human eye sensitivity curve (Figure S8, Supporting Information).[4a,30] It is a challenge to realize this goal in one phosphor, as a balance of low-energy and high-energy emission is needed. Thus, to design novel white-light MOFs simply from the combination of metals and ligands is quite difficult. Other reliable strategies

should be explored. Most recently, encapsulating yellow- or green/red-emitting guests (lanthanide ions, complexes, or dyes) into blue-emission MOFs have been proven a good methodology to design and prepare single-phase white-light phosphors. In these examples, efficient blue-emission of a MOF host is a requirement. Nonetheless, in order to achieve white light, the yellow, or green/red-emitting from encapsulants must cater to the blue emission of the MOF host, which restricts the adjustability and affects the quantum efficiency to a certain degree. This problem can be mitigated by introducing strong blue fluorescent guest molecule into a MOF in company with green- and red-emitting guests. In this regard, we tried to introduce three red-green-blue-emitting dyes into a MOF for producing the single-phase white-light phosphor.

HSB-W1>DCM, HSB-W1>C6, and HSB-W1>CBS-127 have proven to be good red, green, and blue emitters, respectively. Thus, if DCM, C6, and CBS-127 are included in HSB-W1 compatibly, white light can be realized through balancing the contents of their emissions. Encouragingly, the MOF composite, HSB-W1>DCM/C6/CBS-127, was obtained simply by soaking HSB-W1 into a mixed DMF solution of DCM, C6, and CBS-127. And the amount of each component can be adjusted by
