changing its relative solution concentration. According to the screening tests, HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6/CBS-127 (0.003 wt% DCM, 0.002 wt% C6, 0.0005 wt% CBS-127), which are capable of emitting white light, has been synthesized. Upon exciting at \(365\,\mathrm{\nm}\) , the emission spectrum covers almost the whole visible spectral region, with the CIE chromaticity coordinates measured as (0.33, 0.33), the same to that of the ideal white light (Figure 4a). The CRI and CCT values were determined to be 80 and \(5553\;\mathrm{~K~}\) , respectively. The quantum yield was \(20.2\%\) , measured at room temperature by the means of an integrating sphere. Although CRI 80 is ready for human eye-friendly application, the value should be further enhanced, as the higher value gives the better color rendering ability. We therefore increased the contents of DCM, C6, and CBS-127 in HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6/CBS-127 by raising their concentrations of soak solution. When the amounts of DCM, C6, and CBS-127 were all \(0.03\,\mathrm{\DeltaWt\%}\) , a better quality of white light was generated. As seen in Figure 4b, a broad emission spectrum threads throughout the visible light range when exciting at \(365\,\mathrm{\nm}\) , with CIE coordinates of (0.33, 0.32). The CRI and CCT values were 87 and \(5440\;\mathrm{~K~}\) , respectively. Meanwhile, the quantum yield was also improved to \(24.0\%\) \((\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=365\ \mathrm{nm})\) ). Furthermore, the white light can keep when varying the excitation wavelength from 325 to \(390~\mathrm{nm}\) , which is beneficial to WLED devices. For comparison, HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6 (0.005 wt% DCM, \(0.001\%\) wt% C6) was prepared by simultaneously incorporated red and green dyes (DCM and C6) into HSB-W1, which also emitted white light under excitation at \(365~\mathrm{nm}\) , with CIE coordinates of (0.28, 0.30). And the CRI, quantum yield, and CCT values were 87, \(17.6\%\) , and \(9564~\mathrm{K}\) , respectively. It can be seen that the overall performance of white-light output is not as good as that of HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6/CBS-127, and the white-light emission changed greatly when varying the excitation wavelength slightly (Figure S9, Supporting Information).

To examine the universality of our strategy and further improve the white-light properties, other fluorochrome guests were tested. First, we changed the green-emission component C6 with C6a. As expected, novel composite HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6a/CBS-127 was prepared. The amount of each guest can be tuned according to requirements.

HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6a/CBS-127 (0.03 wt% DCM, 0.02 wt% C6a, 0.02 wt % CBS-127) displayed white light, with CIE coordinates, CRI, CCT, and quantum yield values of (0.31, 0.32), 90, \(6638\ \mathrm{K}\) , and \(26.0\%\) , respectively, when exciting at \(365~\mathrm{nm}\) . Likewise, HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6a/CBS-127 always emitted white light when changing the excitation wavelengths from 300 to \(385\,\mathrm{\nm}\) (Figure 5a,b). After storing the HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6a/ CBS-127 composite for about 12 weeks, it still emitted good white light (Figure S10, Supporting Information). In comparison with previously reported white-light-emitting MOF composites (Table 1), the CRI and quantum yield values of HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6a/CBS-127 are among the highest level.

Subsequently, the red-emitting component DCM was changed by DSM, which was primarily used in the Qian's system.[25] HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6/CBS-127 (0.02 wt% DSM, 0.04 wt% C6, 0.03 wt% CBS-127) under excitation of \(365~\mathrm{nm}\) light sent out broadband white light with CIE coordinates of (0.31, 0.32) (Figure 5c,d). The CRI value is as high as 92, representing one of the highest CRI values among the reported white-light-emitting MOF materials. The CCT is \(7494\,\mathrm{~K~}\) , and quantum yield is \(15.6\%\) . The blue-light component CBS-127 can be also replaced by KSN. As a result, HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DSM/ C6/KSN (0.02 wt% DSM, 0.01 wt% C6, 0.01 wt% KSN) was obtained, which also gave out white light when stimulated with UV light. Under excitation at \(330~\mathrm{nm}\) , the CIE, CCT, and CRI values were (0.32, 0.32), 6016 K, and 91, respectively. When changing the excitation wavelength to \(385~\mathrm{nm}\) , the CIE, CCT, CRI, and quantum yield values became (0.30, 0.31), 7829 K, 92, and \(19.3\%\) , respectively (Figure 5c,d).

Furthermore, the potential of three red/green/blue-emitting dyes-contained MOFs for practical white-light emission application has been demonstrated. A rough WLED was fabricated by simply coating the HSB-W1 \(\rightharpoondown\) DCM/C6/CBS-127 (0.03 wt% DCM, 0.03 wt% C6, 0.03 wt% CBS-127) solid on the surface of a commercially available ultraviolet LED chip (3 mm, 365 nm). The resultant WLED device emitted bright white light at a voltage of \(3.8~\mathrm{V}\) , as illustrated in Figure 6. Therefore, three red/ green/blue-emitting dyes-contained MOFs, serving as novel single-phase white-light phosphor materials, are potential for practical lighting application.
