In summary, a variety of neutral and ionic guest molecules, such as fluorescent brighteners, coumarin derivatives, DCM, and DSM, have been encapsulated in a neutral MOF, HSB-W1, yielding novel blue-, green-, and red-phosphors, respectively. In addition, to design and prepare the white-light-emitting MOF materials, the combined three red/green/blue-emitting dyes were introduced simultaneously into such MOF host for the first time. As a result, four composites, HSB-W1>DCM/C6/CBS-127, HSB-W1>DCM/C6a/CBS-127, HSB-W1>DSM/C6/CBS-127, and HSB-W1>DSM/C6/KSN, have been obtained, which illuminate white light upon excitation with UV light, with nearly ideal CIE coordinates, high CRI and quantum yields, and moderate CCT values. The white light is tunable just through changing the contents or types of the three dye guests, or the excitation wavelength. Importantly, the introduction of blue-emitting guests in our methodology makes the available MOF host more extensive, and the final white-light output more tunable and higher quality. As there are so many porous MOFs and various fluorescent molecules, lots of white-light-emitting composites could be obtained if they are combined efficiently. Employing this strategy, some novel WLED with practical lighting application will appear in the future.
